NekoKEM Linux CLI 4.1.0
======================

This archive contains the standalone NekoKEM Linux CLI for the architecture
named by the archive. The executable is a static PIE and includes its OpenSSL
4.0.3 cryptographic runtime, so no OpenSSL or other shared-library package is
required to run it.

Automatic installation
----------------------

Download and inspect the installer, then run it:

    curl --fail --location --output install.sh \
        https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Shixiaoshi0417/NekoKEM/main/linux/install.sh
    less install.sh
    sh install.sh

The installer detects x86_64 or aarch64, downloads the matching archive from
the fixed v4.1.0 GitHub Release, verifies the archive's SHA256SUMS, and
installs nekokem in /usr/local/bin. It uses sudo only when the destination is
not writable; if sudo is unavailable, it prints a manual recovery command.
The installer never compiles source code or installs OpenSSL or other packages.
SHA-256 detects archive corruption but does not prove its publisher. For releases
after v4.1.0 that include attestations, install GitHub CLI and require provenance
verification with:

    NEKOKEM_VERIFY_ATTESTATION=1 sh install.sh

This runs gh attestation verify on the archive before extraction and installation.
A failed or unavailable attestation stops installation. The pinned v4.1.0
release predates these attestations, so enabling this option for that release
will stop installation until a verified attestation is available.

After installation:

    nekokem --version

NekoKEM is an experimental file-encryption project. It has not received an
independent professional security audit and should not be used for formally
regulated workloads or high-value, long-term data protection.

Verify the package contents
---------------------------

From this directory, run:

    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS

Basic use
---------

Generate an encrypted Hybrid private key and matching public key:

    ./nekokem keygen

Encrypt with the default NKEM v3 Hybrid protocol:

    ./nekokem encrypt hybrid input.bin output.nkem keys/public.key

Decrypt an NKEM v3 Hybrid container:

    ./nekokem decrypt hybrid output.nkem recovered.bin keys/private.key.enc

The key-establishment construction is X448 + ML-KEM-1024, followed by
HKDF-SHA512 and AES-256-GCM. One recipient uses NKEM v3; two or more recipient
public keys use NKEM v4. NKEM v1 and v2 containers are rejected. NKPR private-key storage is unchanged.

The default interactive interface creates keys/, plaintext/, and encrypted/
under the current working directory. Keep private-key passwords and generated
key files secure. Interactive decryption refuses an existing plaintext output;
move or remove that file before retrying. A destination created during password
entry is also refused atomically. Exiting with option 5 or EOF returns
the result of the most recent operation (success before any operation).

New passwords must be valid UTF-8; existing passwords retain their original
byte interpretation. A Windows mintty/MSYS pseudoterminal that cannot reliably
hide secret input is refused; use Windows Terminal or redirected stdin.
POSIX startup disables core dumps; Linux also disables process dumpability.
Pasted keys use an owner-only 0700 directory under XDG_RUNTIME_DIR when it is
safe, otherwise ~/.nekokem-tmp, and are not fsynced. Signal cleanup still removes
pasted files. Abrupt termination such as SIGKILL can leave a temporary file;
remove only your own nekokem-paste.* directories after verifying no process uses them.
Encrypted PKCS#8 PEM paste is rejected immediately; select an NKPR file instead.
Displayed paths replace terminal controls, bidi controls and zero-width characters
with ?. The original path remains unchanged for file operations.

